[THOUGHT] The Analyst has twice indicated troponin data is unavailable or not reportable in this format — likely meaning the value isn’t accessible in the current clinical environment. Per protocol, I must not repeat the request and instead pivot to another high-yield, available test. Given bilateral rales, dyspnea, and chest pain with normal ECG and no clear cardiac history, the next most informative tests are:  
- Chest X-ray (to confirm pulmonary edema, interstitial/alveolar pattern, cardiomegaly, pleural effusions, or alternative findings like pneumonia or pneumothorax);  
- Arterial blood gas (to assess oxygenation, acid-base status — e.g., respiratory alkalosis in PE or metabolic acidosis in shock);  
- BNP or NT-proBNP (to support heart failure vs. other causes of rales).  
Among these, CXR is widely available, rapid, and directly visualizes the rales’ structural correlate. It’s listed under “Available tests” in prior Analyst replies (implied by context), and will help distinguish cardiogenic pulmonary edema from pneumonia, aspiration, or ARDS.

[TEST] Chest X-ray (PA and lateral views)

